uh all right so guys here's my talk at  the table it's about worker burnout  and fatigue during the pandemic so last  month the jobs website  monster found 69 of employees reported  burnout while working from home  during the pandemic that is up 35  from may this comes as the national  unemployment rate fell to 10.2  in july which is still historically high  psychiatrist dr sue varma joins us now  to talk about the challenges  and how to stop the stigma surrounding  mental health  sue it's so good to see you thank you so  much for joining us let me ask you  right off the bat what is burnout what  is  work burnout and why do people feel more  vulnerable now  so burnout is this unique combination of  feeling  exhausted from your job having low  energy  having a negative mood or feeling  cynical about it  and not really feeling as if you have  efficacy or  agency or that your work matters anymore  and as you said you know two-thirds of  people are feeling this right now  and we're seeing that this is even more  so in the pandemic for a variety of  reasons we're not used to working  from home for this long and and and let  me say that there are some countries you  know in sweden they say that 30  of people work remotely and that's the  norm and that's the baseline  and trust me there's some people who  thrive but i have a lot of folks telling  me that this is  long and that they're missing the human  connection that they would normally get  so what causes burnout i sometimes get  the sense  that uh that the ground beneath me  is uneven like i'm sort of walking on  quicksand or on muddy  on a muddy ground like this the ground  is always shifting is that a way to you  know that's how i feel so  so what causes that feeling yes  so sometimes it's a feeling of uh that  there's more work  coming in that you can handle you know i  know that there are a lot of people who  are experiencing  a variety of family pressures whether it  be if you're at home and you're taking  care of a family and you're managing  remote learning  or maybe you're managing somebody who's  been sick in your family when  what's being thrown at you is more than  you can handle and that's really what  the definition when we talk about  workplace burnout and  you know i want to say that this is a  condition it's not necessarily a  disorder  it was recognized by the world health  organization earlier this year  and what we're seeing is that it is  unmanageable or  a stress that's not managed properly so  stress is normal we're all going to  experience  days where you know we feel like we're  burning the candle on both edges  but there's something about the quality  that we're not able to live up to or  meet the demands and a lot of times this  happens because our bosses  are employers they don't know what we're  contending with at home they're not  aware or maybe not sensitive to the  personal life aspect  and they're not making and we're not  making time for ourselves and taking  breaks  so all of this the external pressures at  work and then the personal demands  um you know there's not a balance and  there's an overflow in the system if you  will  you know we've talked about this before  sue i think where you know i told you  once that  what i try to do when i'm feeling  stressed is i think about the places  that i've been as a reporter  where i've seen people who are really  struggling with their situation who've  lived through  insurmountable tragedies and i think  about people who live through jim crow  or the great depression and i try to put  my life  in context to that but i don't know if  that's always the right thing to do to  look at somebody else or another  situation and say  well it's not as bad as that  yes it's a good point because for some  people and i'm glad that that works for  you and i think it does for some people  but for other people they say well you  know what it feels invalidating or it  feels minimizing  you know i know that you know if i  complain to my husband he'll be like but  you know what look at all these other  positive things  and sometimes what we need in that  moment is really someone to validate us  to empathize and to reassure us and to  be able to have  a conversation and that conversation  often should be with people that we work  with to say listen  this is what i have on my plate is there  any way that we can redistribute this  i'm going through a hard time right now  i think it's a lot it's hard for people  to say people want to show up and  especially in this pandemic when we  don't have  the daily check-ins i mean there were so  many built-in things that you could just  turn to a co-worker and say hey how  would you manage this  now it requires a phone call or a  meeting and so what i'm hearing is  people  saying that what they're missing with  the remote working is number one the  connections and the micro connections  that were built in you know the water  cooler conversations that you didn't  have to make an effort  or slacking someone on a work computer  system and really having an easy  dialogue  and they're missing what's also  interesting vlad is the commute  now i don't think anyone in a million  years would tell you oh i miss being on  the train  but we're finding that studies are  showing that being on the train  or having some sort of a commute even if  you're walking or biking to work  was allowed you emotional processing  time it was allowing you to get ready  for your day and then it was allowing  you to decompress  so you had these natural transitions  we're finding that people are working on  average an hour and a half to two hours  longer i know i was up on  my computer late last night and people  are sending you emails and we don't have  the same sort of boundaries because  our work day doesn't have a finite  ending the way it used to  that's a great point uh dr sue varma  thank you i encourage our viewers to  check out your  instagram your social media you always  have words of wisdom  there thank you very much sue we  appreciate it
 

